> Derek Ray wrote:
>> Does this encourage the player to do anything other than sit in a
>> closet, type "1000.", and eat food occasionally?  Generally by the time
>> the player starts seeing flayers, food is long since a moot point.
>
> In my games, I believe* I have encountered at least as many flayers on the
> ascension run (due in part to summon nasties) as before waking the wizard,
> and at that point of the game, hanging around waiting is not a great
> option.

I can say that in vanilla, I encounter zero flayers on the ascension run
unless they happen to be summon-nastied from Rodney, and if they do I teleport
them away, death them, or Elbereth and go around them.  If they aren't
in range for melee, I teleport myself past them.  I don't fight things
during the run in vanilla except for Rodney; it just leaves Rodney more
turns to possibly show up and get in the way.

75% of the time I've genocided them, so this makes my perspective a bit
tricky, admittedly.  But this is my response to titans and Archons as
well (teleport 'em away), and neither of those are genoable, so I don't see
that I'd change a working strategy for another "owie dangerous" critter.

> As for before the ascension run, yes, some players may prefer to wait out
> their amnesia in a closet, but I had intended that knowledge would
> gradually return over those 1000 turns, so that you could still most likely
> carry on with your game, waiting only for critical items to be remembered.

Right, but why would you?  Either you forgot non-critical items, in which
case the attack was meaningless, or you lost critical items, in which
case you sit in a closet and wait.  Penalizes low-turn ascenders, but
did those guys really need to be whacked in the shins?  (note difference
between low-turn and low-real-time).

> Furthermore, starting with 1000 turns of amnesia for the first successful
> attack makes single brain sucks less troubling, but doubling the
> time-until-remembrance with each attack could land you with 16000 turns of
> amnesia if a master mind flayer gets all its attacks in, and I'm not sure
> waiting that long is entirely trivial. It would be quicker to remap and
> reidentify.

There is this, as well, though that's a case of magnitude rather than
the effect itself -- can always have the amnesia 'cap' at a certain turn
max, and have later effects re-increase it but only to that max.

>> (It wouldn't be particularly difficult, i'm just trying to see whether
>> it would end up mattering that much).
>
> I think the major effect would be that an easy early-game problem (solved by
> waiting) becomes a difficult end-game problem, which fits in with the aims
> of SporkHack, doesn't it?

If that was what I expected the effect to be, then yes.  But I'm not
sure that this actually creates difficulty at the right point for the
player... or that it changes flayers away from their current 'Geno
Unless Conduct' status.

> [*] If I believe incorrectly, then you could just tweak the flayer
> generation probability so that more really do appear on the ascension run.

Doing this just puts them back on the geno list.  I can't afford to have
large quantities of resources suddenly go unavailable on the run back
up, _especially_ so in Spork where Rodney is a real bastard to Lawfuls
and Neutrals.

